-- Round 008 (2026-08-22) — P2 post-review repairs. The adversarial review of
-- the round-007 implementation returned 1 major + 7 minor findings; every one
-- is either repaired here (R1-R6) or closed with an explicit reason. Repairs
-- are registered AS repairs (assert-only, never retract): round-007's actuals
-- stand as the record of the pre-review state; this round records what the
-- review changed and the final re-verified numbers.
--
-- Review findings → dispositions:
--   MAJOR  path-dependent edge Assertion provenance ..... REPAIRED (R1)
--   minor  duplicate attr fid on privileged blob keys .... REPAIRED (R2)
--   minor  no tombstone coverage in the differential ..... REPAIRED (R3)
--   minor  stats trait-doc drift + sha panic contract .... REPAIRED (R4)
--   minor  review-environment integrity .................. VERIFIED (R5)
--   minor  round-007 gate clause stale suite count ....... REPAIRED (R6, 1503→1504
--            in the then-uncommitted round-007.rofl — an edit before first commit,
--            not a retraction of committed ledger)
--   minor  review scratch file in the worktree ........... ALREADY GONE (the review
--            agent deleted tests/rofl_p2_review_probe.rs; verified absent)
--   minor  harness report files restored by reviewer ..... RE-RUN + restored (R5)

-- ── R1 (MAJOR): edge fast paths made winner-coherent — C22's edge sibling ──
-- The review empirically demonstrated (scratch integration probe): after two
-- commits of the SAME edge key (1,2,CALLS) with authors writer-v1/tick 1 then
-- writer-v2/tick 9, `sorted_run(edge)` projected writer-v2/9 while
-- `prefix_scan(edge, [Id(1)])` projected writer-v1/1 — the SAME fid with two
-- different Assertions depending on the read path. Mechanism (read from code):
-- `get_outgoing_edges_at`/`get_incoming_edges_at` scan L0 in FORWARD descriptor
-- order («matching the live shard») so the OLDEST record of a re-asserted key
-- wins their dedup, while `iter_all_edges_at` scans L0 in REVERSE so the NEWEST
-- wins. Exactly the §2.3-family read-path split C22 fixed for nodes, unapplied
-- to edges because the differential compared only (src,dst,type) reprs and no
-- fixture re-committed an edge key.
-- REPAIR (mirrors the C22 treatment):
--   * storage_v2/multi_shard.rs gains `get_edge_at(snap, src, dst, type)` — a
--     newest-wins, tombstone-checked, bloom-gated POINT lookup, the edge twin
--     of `get_node_at` (which the node repair already leaned on). READ-ONLY
--     addition: no writer/format code touched. This refines round-007 H3's
--     evidence wording («no storage_v2 hunk at all») — the GATE clause was and
--     remains «no format-writer changes», which still holds: the hunk is
--     +67 lines of read path, `git diff` shows no writer/segment/manifest hunk.
--   * facts/lsm.rs `edge_winner`: all four keyed edge/incoming fast paths
--     re-resolve every deduped candidate through the point path before
--     synthesis, so one fid always yields one Assertion (prefix_scan is a
--     WINDOW of sorted_run at FactRow level, provenance included).
--   * differential upgraded: `read_differential` now builds a full-run
--     fid → (author, tick) map over ALL FIVE relations and checks EVERY keyed
--     probe (pairs 2/4/5/6/7) against it — cross-path provenance can never go
--     structurally unobserved again.
--   * fixtures: facts::lsm::tests::edge_provenance_is_path_independent_after_reassert
--     (the review's exact reproduction, all four keyed paths) and
--     facts::differential::differential_pairs_after_edge_reassert (full battery
--     over a re-asserted key, plus sha-invariance across FullMerge compaction —
--     compaction's own dedup keeps the newest record, verified by sha equality).
--   * MUTATION-TESTED: with `edge_winner` removed from the forward fast path,
--     both new tests FAIL (0 passed / 1 failed each); with it restored, green.
-- Note inherited legacy-write behavior surfaced while building the repro: the
-- delegated write path (`upsert_edges`) routes an edge to its SOURCE node's
-- shard and SILENTLY SKIPS edges whose source node is unknown to the store
-- (tracing::warn only). P2's assert_batch inherits this by delegation — a
-- FactGroup::Edge for a never-committed src commits Ok but stores nothing.
-- Both write paths (legacy and fact) share the behavior identically, so the
-- commutativity square is unaffected; registered here so the P6 converter (and
-- any pre-P6 consumer) knows the write precondition: edge groups require their
-- src node to exist. Today's producers always satisfy it (nodes commit with
-- their edges).
claim_of[a](r1_edge_fast_paths_winner_coherent_c22_sibling).
ground[a](r1_edge_fast_paths_winner_coherent_c22_sibling, review_repro_writer_v1_vs_v2_same_fid_mechanism_l0_scan_order_split).

-- ── R2: privileged blob keys excluded from the attr projection ──
-- attr_rows_for pushed attr(id,'name',X) from the record COLUMN and again from
-- a metadata-blob 'name' key (RESERVED_META_KEYS covered only _source/
-- _generation) — an equal-value collision minted the SAME fid twice in one run
-- (violating §2.2 «no duplicates by construction», double-counting stats() and
-- canonical_state_sha), and pair 7 was structurally blind because its expected
-- set used the identical double-push rule. Legacy blobs DO carry these keys
-- (the JS reader's _parseNode strips type/id/name/file/exported on read for
-- exactly this reason).
-- REPAIR: COLUMN_META_KEYS = {name, file, semantic_id} — blob copies are
-- skipped; the record columns are authoritative, which is today's dispatch
-- precedence in evidence: `attr_to_query`/`snapshot_nodes_by_attr` map
-- name/file onto the record-column filter slots (storage_glue.rs), the
-- executor's `attr` builtin serves them from `first_class_attr` COLUMNS
-- (builtin.rs), and the JS surface strips the blob copies on parse. The
-- differential's pair-7 expected set applies the same precedence (grounded in
-- those dispatch sites, no longer a mirror of the implementation's own loop),
-- and the handcrafted corpus gains node 66 whose blob carries an EQUAL 'file'
-- copy and DIFFERING 'name'/'semantic_id' copies; the unit fixture
-- facts::lsm::tests::attr_blob_collision_with_column_is_single_fact pins
-- single-row-per-fid + column-wins for both variants and unique fids across
-- the full attr run. Scope note: blob keys OUTSIDE the column set (e.g. a blob
-- 'type'/'id'/'exported') still project as attr facts — they mint no duplicate
-- fid (no column-side push exists for them) and match the pair-7 oracle
-- definition; the deeper multi-surface disagreement (attr=columns vs
-- node_attr=blob-verbatim vs nodes_by_attr=mixed) is today's storage reality,
-- owned by the P6 converter's vocabulary, not projectable-away in P2.
claim_of[a](r2_column_authoritative_attr_projection_no_duplicate_fids).
ground[a](r2_column_authoritative_attr_projection_no_duplicate_fids, dispatch_precedence_read_from_attr_to_query_and_first_class_attr).

-- ── R3: tombstone / delete-then-re-add differential coverage ──
-- Every fixture commit was additive (changed_files = []); tombstoned records,
-- delete-then-re-add of the same id, and tombstoned-edge interplay were only
-- incidentally covered by the one-shot real-base run (weak by construction:
-- both sides share the tombstone-checking code).
-- REPAIR: facts::differential::differential_pairs_after_tombstone_and_readd —
-- commit the corpus, reanalyze a/f.js (non-empty changed_files: nodes 11/22 +
-- their outgoing edges tombstoned), re-add id 11 with a DIFFERENT type
-- (FUNCTION→METHOD) and fresh provenance + a new edge, then: explicit probes
-- (22 gone; 11 carries the re-added type AND the re-added record's
-- author/tick; 11→22 dead; 11→44 live), the oracle-leak pin (the re-added
-- id's OLD record leaks into scan_nodes_by_type — its id was UN-tombstoned by
-- the re-add — while count_by_type_at and the fact side agree FUNCTION is
-- extinct), the FULL 12-pair battery across the tombstone generation, sha
-- moved by the tombstone commit (truth) and invariant across a subsequent
-- FullMerge (physics). Runs non-ignored in cargo test --lib.
claim_of[a](r3_tombstone_and_readd_pinned_at_fixture_level).
ground[a](r3_tombstone_and_readd_pinned_at_fixture_level, non_empty_changed_files_battery_plus_untombstoned_old_record_leak_pin).

-- ── R4: doc drift + panic contract made consistent ──
-- (a) FactStore::stats trait doc said «construction-time snapshot counts»
-- while the implementation (and C20, which refines C13) computes at FIRST
-- stats() observation — the trait doc now states the first-observation
-- semantics. (b) canonical_state_sha's expect() on a foreign snapshot is now
-- CONTRACTUAL in the trait doc: a foreign backend's snapshot is a programming
-- error, and unlike get_fact/tuple (whose None legitimately means «absent
-- fact»), a state sha has no honest degraded answer — returning anything would
-- be a wrong hash presented as right. The panic site carries the same note.
claim_of[a](r4_stats_doc_and_sha_panic_contract_aligned).
ground[a](r4_stats_doc_and_sha_panic_contract_aligned, trait_docs_state_first_observation_and_contractual_panic).

